# syntactic_boundary.ps — what a CFG-constrained decoder CANNOT exclude.
#
# THIS FILE IS DELIBERATE NONSENSE. It is a fixture, not an example to copy.
#
# Every line below is semantically meaningless, yet this file is:
#   1. ACCEPTED by grammar/pyths.lark          (the constrained-decoding gate)
#   2. ACCEPTED by `pyths check --syntax-only` (the authoritative parser)
#   3. ACCEPTED by `pyths check`               (parser + type checker: NO ERRORS)
#   4. COMPILED by `pyths compile`             (emits valid JS)
#   5. ...and only then does it fail, at RUNTIME, under node.
#
# Reproduce the whole chain:  python examples/constrained-decoding/syntactic_boundary.py
#
# The point: grammar-constrained decoding guarantees that the emitted program
# PARSES. It guarantees nothing else. A context-free grammar cannot express
# "the operands of `-` have compatible types" or "this name is bound" — those
# are not properties of the token string. Nor, as (3) shows, does PythScribe's
# type checker close the gap: it is PARTIAL (it catches e.g. arity, but none of
# the below). So "the model cannot emit a malformed program" means malFORMED,
# not meaningless.

# a string minus an int -> compiles to pySub("hello", 3)
x = "hello" - 3

# an attribute on None -> compiles to y.nonexistent_method(1, 2)
y = None
z = y.nonexistent_method(1, 2)

# an unbound name -> compiles to pyAdd(a, undefined_global)
def f(a):
    return a + undefined_global

# calling a non-callable -> compiles to w()
w = 5
w()

# a string times a string -> compiles to pyMul("text", "text")
result = "text" * "text"

print(x)
